.TH YESH-LANGUAGE 7 "October 2026" "yesh" "Yesh Language Manual"
.SH NAME
yesh-language \- language reference for the yesh Unix automation language
.SH PRINCIPLES
yesh has one preferred way to express a common operation. It keeps strings,
lists, regular expressions and command arguments distinct. It does not require
.B $,
historical test operators, or shell word-splitting rules.
.SH LEXICAL RULES
.IP \(bu 2
Indentation uses spaces; tabs are rejected.
.IP \(bu 2
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
Double-quoted strings use Go-style escapes.
.IP \(bu 2
Single-quoted strings preserve their contents and are recommended for regex.
.SH VALUES
.SS Scalars
.EX
name = web-1
message = "hello world"
.EE
.SS Lists
.EX
hosts = [web-1, web-2, web-3]
.EE
.PP
Nested lists are not supported in the current runtime.
.SS Regular expressions
.EX
unit = regex '^(api|worker)@[a-z0-9.-]+$'
.EE
.PP
The pattern is compiled when assigned. Invalid patterns and duplicate named
capture groups are errors.
.SH CONTROL FLOW
.SS if
.EX
if exists "/etc/myapp.conf":
    echo "configured"
else:
    echo "missing"
.EE
.PP
An external command can be used as a condition. Exit status zero selects the
body; a normal non-zero exit status selects
.B else.
Permission failures, symlink loops, signals and timeouts are execution errors,
not ordinary false conditions. Use only exists path; exists(path) is rejected.
.SS exit
.EX
exit 2
.EE
.PP
exit $code terminates the entire script with an explicit decimal code from
0 to 255, including inside nested blocks. It is not a pipeline stage.
In plan mode exit is displayed without terminating plan traversal.
.SS matches
.EX
name = api-42
pattern = regex '^api-[0-9]+$'
if $name matches $pattern:
    echo "valid"
.EE
.SS for
.EX
for host in $hosts:
    ssh $host systemctl is-active api
.EE
.PP
Only finite lists are accepted. The runtime enforces iteration and total-step
limits. The
.B while
statement is disabled.
.SS with lock
.EX
with lock /var/lib/yesh/backup.lock:
    rsync -a source/ temporary/
    mv -- temporary current
.EE
.PP
On Linux this takes an exclusive non-blocking advisory lock for the indented
body and releases it on every exit path. In plan mode it is only displayed.
.SS publish
.EX
publish temporary to current
.EE
.PP
publish uses a same-filesystem rename and fails on a cross-filesystem move;
it does not silently copy. It provides namespace atomicity, not crash
durability without explicit fsync policy.
.SS retention plan
.EX
rsync retention plan in $root keep $keep
.EE
.PP
This read-only operation lists completed snapshot directories older than the
newest keep entries. A candidate must be a real directory named
.B snapshot.<number>;
staging names and symlinks are not accepted. The keep count must be between 1
and 10000. The report labels newest entries
.B KEEP
and older entries
.B PRUNE;
the latter is only a report label. The operation never removes or renames anything, including in
normal execution; it is a preview primitive. Use the CLI
.B --plan
mode when the surrounding script must also avoid external commands and other
filesystem changes.
.SS retention quarantine
.EX
with lock $lock:
    rsync retention quarantine in $root keep $keep to $quarantine
.EE
.PP
The quarantine directory must already exist as a real direct child of the
retention root. The operation moves completed
.B snapshot.<number>
directories older than
.B keep
with rename inside the explicit lock. It never deletes quarantine contents and
has no copy fallback across filesystems. In
.B --plan
mode it reports candidates without changing the filesystem.
.SS Read-only storage (Linux)
.nf
backup = device by uuid "replace-with-real-uuid"
inspect $backup
require mounted /mnt/backup from $backup filesystem "ext4"
.fi
.PP
Use uuid or partuuid for an exact, unique identity. inspect prints the lookup
snapshot as JSON. require mounted repeats lookup and checks the exact directory,
device major:minor and filesystem; a directory merely contained in the root
mount fails. Symlink mountpoints and filesystem subtrees are rejected.
Whole-filesystem bind mounts cannot be distinguished. These are snapshots,
not protection against concurrent device/mount changes or a write permission test.
.PP
Queries require lsblk and findmnt (util-linux), have a ten-second execution limit plus a two-second cancellation grace period,
and accept at most 4 MiB per output stream. Missing/ambiguous identities and
query failures stop execution. Device values cannot become command arguments.
Plan mode defers all storage queries and path checks. Mount/unmount
are not implemented by this typed interface.
.SS fstab snapshots (Linux)
.nf
mounts = fstab "/etc/fstab"
inspect $mounts
.fi
.PP
Reads a regular-file snapshot through findmnt/libmount without device evaluation,
path canonicalization or mounting. Parser diagnostics fail the query even with
exit status zero. Empty files are valid. Limits: 4 MiB input, 64 KiB per line,
10,000 records; the private temporary snapshot is removed after the query.
.PP
Inspection prints ordered entries, dump/fsck numbers and duplicate-target
diagnostics. Swap entries sharing none are not duplicate targets. Option values
and non-local sources are hidden to avoid printing credentials; option order
and values are retained internally. Plan defers all reading.
.PP
This is configuration, not active mount or boot verification. nofail does not
prove storage readiness; noauto does not disable x-systemd.automount. systemd
handles passno differently from traditional fsck ordering. No fstab writing,
mount helpers, daemon-reload or systemd generator execution is performed.
.PP
To bind one configuration entry to its device and exact active mount:
.nf
mounts = fstab "/etc/fstab"
require mounted /mnt/backup from $mounts
.fi
.PP
Exactly one entry (including lexically equivalent paths) must match. Its
UUID/PARTUUID must resolve uniquely, and its explicit filesystem type and device
must match the kernel mount table. UUID/PARTUUID tags may have enclosing quotes.
Swap, path/LABEL sources, auto/type lists, bind/rbind, loop, move/remount and
btrfs subvolume options are unsupported and fail explicitly.
.PP
Kernel lookup disables canonicalization and precedes directory inspection;
an autofs-only target fails before traversal. nofail, noauto and automount
options never excuse a missing mount. Plan defers entry/device/mount checks.
The loaded fstab snapshot is used; later edits are not reread. This verifies
mountpoint, device and filesystem, not options, write access, free space or boot.
Concurrent mount/device changes still require an external boundary.
.SH NETWORK VALUES AND READ-ONLY REPORTS (LINUX)
.SS IP and CIDR values
.EX
address = ip "192.0.2.17"
network = cidr "192.0.2.0/24"
if contains $network $address:
    echo "inside"
.EE
.PP
The current typed subset supports strict IPv4/IPv6 parsing, contains,
overlaps, masked, first, last and address classification. These operations
compute and validate values; they do not change network state. Invalid prefixes,
IPv6 zones in CIDR values and mixed address families are errors.
.SS External network reports
.EX
ip -br address show
ip route show
ip neigh show
ss -H -n -l -t -u
.EE
.PP
These remain ordinary external commands. They use the caller's permissions;
yesh does not add sudo. Active arp-scan discovery is also external and must
receive an explicit interface and IPv4 CIDR. The example checks an executable
path with exists and
reports that Debian/Ubuntu, Rocky and Alma Linux normally provide a package
named arp-scan. It does not install packages, select --localnet implicitly or
scan routed networks.
.PP
Examples must use neutral addresses and MAC values or explicit parameters;
they must not publish identifiers from a real infrastructure. A real positive
ARP result requires a separate host on the same Layer-2 segment.
.SH EXTERNAL DEPENDENCIES AND PRIVILEGE
.PP
A missing executable must stop before the useful operation or select an
explicit dependency-error branch. Package hints are informational. yesh never
invokes sudo or prompts for a password automatically; cron and CI should use
sudo -n when privilege is explicitly required. Local root does not grant root
on a remote host.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands remain Unix commands and are executed without an implicit shell.
Arguments are preserved as separate values.
.EX
grep error app.log | head 20
echo value > output.txt
cat < input.txt
.EE
.PP
The following constructs are rejected by default:
.B ; , & , && , || , $() ,
backticks, nested shell invocation, background jobs, and incomplete pipelines.
.SH ERRORS
Errors include the script filename and line number where available. A failed
external command stops normal execution. Missing executables and process
startup errors are not treated as ordinary false conditions.
.SH DESIGN BOUNDARY
yesh is an administrative scripting language, not a Python replacement and
not a Bash compatibility layer. SSH, rsync, systemctl, journalctl, mount and
other Unix tools remain external commands until a documented typed yesh
operation is added.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR yesh (1)
